WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.113 --> 00:00:01.543
- I growed up.

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:03.800
I was way into years.

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:05.980
We had a outside toilet.

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:07.830
We drawed water out of the well.

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:09.720
We milked cows.

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:12.353
We growed up tough, but we made it.

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:18.380
- [Mark] All right, Randy.
- [Randy] Yes.

00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:20.960
Randy, where'd you grow up,
where are you from originally?

00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:23.360
- I'm from eastern Kentucky,
right here in this old holler.

00:00:23.360 --> 00:00:26.010
Spurlock Fort, Dana, Kentucky.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:27.050
- [Mark] This is Floyd County.

00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:29.460
- This is Floyd County, yes, it is.

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:32.063
I grew up out here on the old farm.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:34.500
- [Mark] Tell me, tell
me about your childhood.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:35.820
You had both your parents, growing up?

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:37.630
- Yes, I had my mother and daddy.

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:41.490
Johnny Acres and Margie
Acres was my mom and dad.

00:00:41.490 --> 00:00:44.610
We growed up, they was seven children.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:46.910
We didn't have any running water.

00:00:46.910 --> 00:00:49.543
We didn't have no bathroom in the house.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:54.150
We drawed water for mom to wash clothes

00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:55.763
on old wringer washer.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:00.693
Dad worked in the coal
mine from daylight to dark.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.120
He raised us here on the farm.

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:06.000
He raised what we eat.

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.030
He raised hogs, chicken, cattle.

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:12.120
We plowed the old garden
with the old mule.

00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:15.070
Me and my brother, when we got old enough,

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:18.470
we plowed, we hoed, we
took care of the farm

00:01:18.470 --> 00:01:19.753
while dad worked.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:23.970
Mom, she was a housewife.

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:27.993
She took care of the house and
all the washing and cleaning.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:32.450
We grew up poor,

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:34.520
but you know that was the best life

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:38.490
we ever had growing up because
we knowed what it meant

00:01:38.490 --> 00:01:39.690
to live.

00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:42.260
You had to work, you
had to raise your food.

00:01:42.260 --> 00:01:44.703
You couldn't run to the store and buy it.

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:49.060
And when we got up in
the, way up in the years,

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:53.940
I remember we got some running
water in the old house.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:56.750
Oh, that was the best
thing ever happened to us

00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:57.853
was running water.

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:03.350
Then they pitched in and
they built mom a bathroom.

00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:05.270
That tickled my mom to death

00:02:05.270 --> 00:02:07.280
because she was never
had nothing like that.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:08.984
- [Mark] You had an
outhouse up until then?

00:02:08.984 --> 00:02:10.520
- We had an outhouse, yes.

00:02:10.520 --> 00:02:11.967
We called it the old toilet.

00:02:11.967 --> 00:02:13.063
- [Mark] And what is an outhouse?

00:02:13.063 --> 00:02:15.110
It's just a place that
you go to the bathroom

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:16.707
and then the water, the waste.

00:02:16.707 --> 00:02:18.950
- The water over it,
the water you build it

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:21.480
over the creek or a
stream is what I call it.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:22.420
- [Mark] So the waste goes into the creek?

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:24.723
- The waste went in the creek.

00:02:25.900 --> 00:02:30.298
And as time grew, you
know, things was different.

00:02:30.298 --> 00:02:33.360
I remember having an
old TV line up the hill.

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:36.470
You got one channel, Channel Three.

00:02:36.470 --> 00:02:38.660
We would go to school during the day,

00:02:38.660 --> 00:02:40.410
we'd come home in the evening,

00:02:40.410 --> 00:02:41.670
that's the first thing we'd do

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:43.530
is hit that hill to turn that antennae

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:45.460
so we can watch Channel Three.

00:02:45.460 --> 00:02:47.070
That's all we had.

00:02:47.070 --> 00:02:51.600
And as time went on,
they finally got TV cable

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:53.543
and phone, and all that stuff.

00:02:53.543 --> 00:02:56.740
I remember the first phone they ever had

00:02:56.740 --> 00:02:58.610
was called a party line.

00:02:58.610 --> 00:03:01.540
You had to get on it first,
because if you didn't,

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:02.853
you didn't get to talk.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:07.980
As time got farther along,

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:09.603
dad had to quit work.

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:15.730
You know, there for
years we struggled again.

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:17.570
But, you know, I can thank God.

00:03:17.570 --> 00:03:19.080
He took care of us.

00:03:19.080 --> 00:03:22.210
He kept us all together as our family.

00:03:22.210 --> 00:03:26.130
We worked hard, but until
today I still work hard.

00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:29.287
I keep my condition
going as my dad asked me.

00:03:29.287 --> 00:03:32.227
"Son, keep buying hogs, killings hogs,

00:03:32.227 --> 00:03:34.510
"raise your family right."

00:03:34.510 --> 00:03:39.220
I've raised three daughters
pretty well on my own.

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:42.116
I'm well satisfied with
the way they turned out.

00:03:42.116 --> 00:03:43.260
- [Mark] With your wife?

00:03:43.260 --> 00:03:47.144
- No, no, I divorced my
wife, I raised my kids.

00:03:47.144 --> 00:03:47.977
- [Mark] By yourself?

00:03:47.977 --> 00:03:48.810
- By myself.

00:03:48.810 --> 00:03:52.950
Which my sister, and my,
their niece, my niece,

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:54.970
they helped me raise them girls.

00:03:54.970 --> 00:03:57.050
But we raised all three of them.

00:03:57.050 --> 00:04:00.970
By now, they know what it means to live.

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:04.930
I try to keep things just
the way that dad had it

00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:06.300
back when I growed up.

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:08.680
- [Mark] So why did, why did
the mom not keep the kids?

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:09.513
That's typically how it goes, right?

00:04:09.513 --> 00:04:12.273
- Well, basically because
the mom got on drugs.

00:04:12.273 --> 00:04:13.106
- [Mark] Oh.

00:04:13.106 --> 00:04:16.880
- And you know, that's
one thing I don't do.

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:19.220
Yes, I've had four back surgeries.

00:04:19.220 --> 00:04:23.080
I'm the first to tell you,
but you never turn to drugs

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:24.920
because drugs will kill you.

00:04:24.920 --> 00:04:26.240
- [Mark] That's how the
drugs kind of took hold here

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:27.073
in this region?

00:04:27.073 --> 00:04:28.910
- I have lost a lot of my best friends

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:31.780
that I went to school
with on account of that.

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:35.300
And you know, I will not turn to that.

00:04:35.300 --> 00:04:38.510
I, like I said, I've
had four back surgeries.

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:41.810
I thank God every day
that I still make it.

00:04:41.810 --> 00:04:42.980
And I don't have to rely.

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:44.480
Yes, I hurt.

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:47.040
But you got to go on with your life.

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:51.193
I tell my daughter,
they work all the time.

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:54.880
The main thing is to take care of yourself

00:04:54.880 --> 00:04:58.520
and make sure that you got food to eat.

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:00.930
Because you know, as the old saying,

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:04.140
we not going to be here all
the time to protect them

00:05:04.140 --> 00:05:06.010
and take care of them.

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:08.430
My daughter lives with me now.

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:11.310
She stays here, but she can kill hogs.

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:13.940
She can kill chickens
just as good as I can,

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:15.630
because I've taught her that.

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:18.560
My other daughter, she lives in Georgetown

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:22.730
and I don't see her as much
as I like to, you know,

00:05:22.730 --> 00:05:25.280
but you've got to go where you can work.

00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:28.060
The work population here is way down.

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:30.050
- [Mark] What kind of jobs
that you've had in your life?

00:05:30.050 --> 00:05:32.303
- I've been a coal miner
all my life, mister.

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:35.303
I worked all my life in the coal mines.

00:05:36.350 --> 00:05:41.350
And I have really done well,
you know, but I got four,

00:05:41.870 --> 00:05:46.853
had four back surgeries, like
I said, and I keep going.

00:05:47.710 --> 00:05:50.953
I tend my garden just like I always did.

00:05:52.119 --> 00:05:54.250
But I'd like, love to go back

00:05:55.810 --> 00:05:57.160
and plow the garden

00:05:57.160 --> 00:06:00.950
with the old horse or the
old mule like we used to.

00:06:00.950 --> 00:06:04.990
But anymore its all about
a rototiller and a tractor.

00:06:04.990 --> 00:06:08.210
But I'd like to get away from
that and go back to that way

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:09.900
I was raised up doing it.

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:11.473
- [Mark] Pretty simple.

00:06:11.473 --> 00:06:15.530
- Because it was a lot
better back then, I thought.

00:06:15.530 --> 00:06:17.050
You know, we worked hard.

00:06:17.050 --> 00:06:20.120
I growed up, I worked all my life.

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:22.720
When I was a young kid, I worked.

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:26.310
And all my brothers and my
sister, they worked, too.

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:27.250
- [Mark] How far do you go in school?

00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:29.823
- I graduated from high
school at Betsy Layne.

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:34.973
I went down here to the
old Prater School to the,

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:40.020
and graduated down there,
I guess from eighth grade.

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:41.910
And then we had to go to Betsy Layne,

00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:45.010
but they closed that
old Prater School down

00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:45.923
down there now.

00:06:47.620 --> 00:06:49.800
- [Mark] What do you think
it is about Appalachia

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:51.410
that makes people here so special?

00:06:51.410 --> 00:06:52.674
Is it the struggle?

00:06:52.674 --> 00:06:53.803
The hard, the hard?

00:06:53.803 --> 00:06:55.410
- I believe it's a struggle.

00:06:55.410 --> 00:06:59.310
You know, people in this
area, they work together.

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:02.280
If I do something, my neighbors help me.

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:04.910
If they do something, I go help them.

00:07:04.910 --> 00:07:08.400
Me and my two buddies down
the road, yes, we raise corn.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:11.210
We plant it by hand
and we pick it by hand.

00:07:11.210 --> 00:07:15.400
But that's how we was raised
up doing stuff like that.

00:07:15.400 --> 00:07:18.583
And you know, like I said,
I enjoy doing that stuff.

00:07:19.720 --> 00:07:20.553
And,

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:22.720
you know, I just,

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:25.240
I just wish we could go back

00:07:25.240 --> 00:07:29.760
and do things different,
you know, growing up.

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:31.760
But, you know, that was the good days.

00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:34.520
We couldn't change
anything if we wanted to.

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:36.840
That's how it was meant to be.

00:07:36.840 --> 00:07:37.980
- [Mark] Do you think all this technology

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:39.650
and all the advancements in the world

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:42.653
have set us off on the wrong course?

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:47.300
- Yeah, I think so.

00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:50.350
I think, you know, it's,
nothing personally,

00:07:50.350 --> 00:07:53.240
but I think when they went to cell phones

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:56.720
and computer stuff, that's
when things went down.

00:07:56.720 --> 00:07:59.260
You know, I could be wrong, I don't know.

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:00.500
But that's just my belief.

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:01.980
I don't own a cell phone.

00:08:01.980 --> 00:08:05.360
I don't own a computer
because in my knowledge,

00:08:05.360 --> 00:08:07.610
I don't know how to work stuff like that.

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:09.580
- [Mark] How old are you?
- I'm 60 years old.

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:11.240
- [Mark] Sixty.

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:12.564
What was the best part of your life?

00:08:12.564 --> 00:08:14.230
The best time of your life?
- Growing up.

00:08:14.230 --> 00:08:15.063
- [Mark] As a kid?

00:08:15.063 --> 00:08:18.070
- Growing up as a kid
and helping kill hogs,

00:08:18.070 --> 00:08:20.320
kill chicken, calves, whatever.

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:22.600
That's how we grow, and raise our garden,

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:25.953
can our canned food,
and raise our potatoes.

00:08:26.790 --> 00:08:29.140
That was my best days of my life.

00:08:29.140 --> 00:08:34.140
Growing up with all my, with
my mom and dad and my brother

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:35.950
and brothers and my sister.

00:08:35.950 --> 00:08:37.763
That that was the good days.

00:08:39.230 --> 00:08:43.840
And you know, me and my
family is still close.

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:47.603
Me and my brother, my sister,
we're still real close.

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:52.600
- [Mark] You'd describe yourself as happy?

00:08:52.600 --> 00:08:53.621
- Oh, yes, yes.

00:08:53.621 --> 00:08:54.454
- [Mark] You said you seem to be.

00:08:54.454 --> 00:08:55.593
- Yes, I'm happy.

00:08:56.870 --> 00:08:58.760
- [Mark] Have you ever left?

00:08:58.760 --> 00:09:01.330
- No, I've never left Kentucky here, no.

00:09:01.330 --> 00:09:03.640
I, you know, I've been on vacation visits,

00:09:03.640 --> 00:09:05.891
but I always want to
come home to Kentucky.

00:09:05.891 --> 00:09:07.440
- [Mark] What happens when
you leave eastern Kentucky?

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:08.273
Do you?

00:09:08.273 --> 00:09:09.120
- I miss it.

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:11.140
It's always, I'm wanting to get home.

00:09:11.140 --> 00:09:11.973
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:09:11.973 --> 00:09:14.493
- Because there's no place
like home is my saying.

00:09:16.130 --> 00:09:19.200
- [Mark] What are the worst
parts about living here?

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:20.033
- Well.

00:09:20.033 --> 00:09:20.970
- [Mark] I mean, did
you, did you have a car

00:09:20.970 --> 00:09:22.490
when you were a kid?

00:09:22.490 --> 00:09:24.280
- No, no, no.

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:25.390
We didn't.

00:09:25.390 --> 00:09:27.020
I was probably 16 years old

00:09:27.020 --> 00:09:29.143
when I first got my first bicycle.

00:09:29.980 --> 00:09:31.180
I was probably, no.

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:33.170
- [Mark] And how would
your parents get around

00:09:33.170 --> 00:09:34.260
or how would you get around?

00:09:34.260 --> 00:09:36.990
- Well, my dad had an old
truck that he drove to work.

00:09:36.990 --> 00:09:38.740
That's how, if we went anywhere,

00:09:38.740 --> 00:09:41.780
we all got in the back and
went in the back of the truck.

00:09:41.780 --> 00:09:43.220
- [Mark] That was when you were 16 though?

00:09:43.220 --> 00:09:44.080
- Yeah.

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:45.150
- [Mark] But I'm saying before that.

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:47.110
- [Randy] Well, that's
how we got around then.

00:09:47.110 --> 00:09:49.170
Or we walked or we rode a horse.

00:09:49.170 --> 00:09:50.003
- [Mark] That's what I'm saying, yeah.

00:09:50.003 --> 00:09:50.918
- [Randy] Yeah.

00:09:50.918 --> 00:09:51.751
- [Mark] So if you didn't have a car,

00:09:51.751 --> 00:09:53.193
you were using a horse.
- [Randy] Yeah, yeah.

00:09:54.517 --> 00:09:57.000
- [Randy] But that was the good days.

00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:58.112
- [Mark] Are you religious?

00:09:58.112 --> 00:10:00.593
- [Randy] I go to church, yes, some.

00:10:01.440 --> 00:10:02.913
But not like I should.

00:10:05.420 --> 00:10:06.300
- [Mark] And you have friends?

00:10:06.300 --> 00:10:08.630
- [Randy] Oh, yes, I
got oodlings of friends.

00:10:08.630 --> 00:10:09.463
- [Mark] Yeah?

00:10:11.290 --> 00:10:12.660
What are you proudest of?

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:15.783
- [Randy] Well, just growing
up and seeing my kids grow up.

00:10:17.470 --> 00:10:18.420
- [Mark] Your kids are doing well?

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:19.253
- [Randy] Oh, yes.

00:10:19.253 --> 00:10:20.890
They real, they do real good.

00:10:20.890 --> 00:10:22.140
Yeah, all three of them.

00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:23.090
All three girls.

00:10:23.090 --> 00:10:24.290
And they do excellent.

00:10:24.290 --> 00:10:26.900
I'm really proud of them,
how they've growed up.

00:10:26.900 --> 00:10:27.770
- [Mark] That's great.

00:10:27.770 --> 00:10:30.720
- [Randy] But they've been
taught to grow up this way.

00:10:30.720 --> 00:10:32.420
- [Mark] Yeah, you set a good role model.

00:10:32.420 --> 00:10:33.253
- [Randy] Huh?

00:10:33.253 --> 00:10:34.086
- [Mark] You were a good role model.

00:10:34.086 --> 00:10:35.730
- [Randy] Yes, everybody tells me that.

00:10:35.730 --> 00:10:38.357
They, a lot of my buddies tell me,

00:10:38.357 --> 00:10:39.980
"You've done great raising them."

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:41.500
You know, when I was a young man,

00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:42.590
I wanted to go out here and do stuff.

00:10:42.590 --> 00:10:45.820
But I had a responsibility - kids.

00:10:45.820 --> 00:10:47.950
That was my responsibility.

00:10:47.950 --> 00:10:49.550
And I took care of them.

00:10:50.691 --> 00:10:52.263
- [Mark] Do you have any regrets in life?

00:10:53.450 --> 00:10:54.350
- [Randy] Not really.

00:10:54.350 --> 00:10:57.670
I just, you know, I enjoyed life

00:10:57.670 --> 00:11:01.390
because you don't know if
it's today or tomorrow,

00:11:01.390 --> 00:11:03.370
you don't know when life's over,

00:11:03.370 --> 00:11:06.440
just, you know, you got to
be ready when it's over.

00:11:07.730 --> 00:11:11.820
- [Mark] And then the poverty
you've endured as a child

00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:13.160
was kind of across the board.

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:14.898
Everybody in this region
is in the same boat.

00:11:14.898 --> 00:11:16.310
- Right.

00:11:16.310 --> 00:11:17.870
- [Mark] So you didn't
really feel poor, did you?

00:11:17.870 --> 00:11:18.703
- No, no.

00:11:18.703 --> 00:11:21.120
We was all just alike.

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:23.810
I tell my kids about today.

00:11:23.810 --> 00:11:25.150
We growed up.

00:11:25.150 --> 00:11:29.480
If you got a pop, it
was a special occasion,

00:11:29.480 --> 00:11:31.114
because they didn't have money to buy it.

00:11:31.114 --> 00:11:34.420
We, me and my brother,

00:11:34.420 --> 00:11:35.890
we milked cows.

00:11:35.890 --> 00:11:39.853
That's how we got our
milk for many, many years.

00:11:42.460 --> 00:11:44.890
And we, you know, like I
said, we had our own chickens,

00:11:44.890 --> 00:11:46.670
we milked our own cows.

00:11:46.670 --> 00:11:47.900
We done it all.

00:11:47.900 --> 00:11:49.397
That's how we growed up.

00:11:49.397 --> 00:11:52.360
That's what make you
respect life, you know,

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:54.510
how you growed up.

00:11:54.510 --> 00:11:56.040
- [Mark] And tell me about
working as a coal miner.

00:11:56.040 --> 00:11:57.650
I mean that, that's a
very rough job, right?

00:11:57.650 --> 00:11:58.540
Dangerous?

00:11:58.540 --> 00:12:00.370
- Well, it's very dangerous.

00:12:00.370 --> 00:12:05.370
I worked six days a week,
10, 12, 14 hours a day

00:12:05.480 --> 00:12:08.910
since I, when I turned 18 years old.

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:10.270
I went to work in the coal mines

00:12:10.270 --> 00:12:11.452
because that's all they was.

00:12:11.452 --> 00:12:12.285
- [Mark] There were no other options.

00:12:12.285 --> 00:12:14.200
- No other options.

00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:17.543
And I worked many, many
years for the same company.

00:12:18.630 --> 00:12:21.770
Like I said, it's about
three, four miles from here,

00:12:21.770 --> 00:12:22.670
you know?

00:12:22.670 --> 00:12:24.140
But it was a great job.

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:27.183
It was great people to work for.

00:12:27.183 --> 00:12:30.440
They treated you with respect.

00:12:30.440 --> 00:12:33.597
You know, I'm sure we made
them millions of dollars

00:12:33.597 --> 00:12:35.253
but that's what we was there for.

00:12:37.318 --> 00:12:38.870
- [Mark] It kept the whole region alive?

00:12:38.870 --> 00:12:39.940
- Oh, yes, yes.

00:12:39.940 --> 00:12:41.060
- [Mark] It's afforded all the businesses,

00:12:41.060 --> 00:12:41.968
all the everything.

00:12:41.968 --> 00:12:42.801
- Yes.

00:12:42.801 --> 00:12:44.050
- [Mark] And now that that's dried up.

00:12:44.050 --> 00:12:46.670
- We had two little grocery
stores here on the creek

00:12:46.670 --> 00:12:49.790
when I growed up and went
to grade school down here.

00:12:49.790 --> 00:12:51.860
You could go and eat lunch there.

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:54.510
They didn't have a lunch
room at our grade school.

00:12:54.510 --> 00:12:56.130
You'd eat lunch.

00:12:56.130 --> 00:12:58.210
Mom and dad had a credit set up.

00:12:58.210 --> 00:13:00.720
You'd get 35 cents worth of stuff.

00:13:00.720 --> 00:13:02.700
But a lot of time for 35 cents,

00:13:02.700 --> 00:13:04.940
you couldn't eat everything you got.

00:13:04.940 --> 00:13:07.930
Now you can't get a pop for 35 cents.

00:13:07.930 --> 00:13:09.973
You know, times has changed.

00:13:11.360 --> 00:13:12.630
- [Mark] Yeah, but the, but the, your,

00:13:12.630 --> 00:13:14.710
the income for this region
is pretty much the same

00:13:14.710 --> 00:13:16.840
as it's been back in the late '70s.

00:13:16.840 --> 00:13:18.500
People don't, people
aren't making more money

00:13:18.500 --> 00:13:20.090
and yet everything costs more.

00:13:20.090 --> 00:13:21.053
- True, very true.

00:13:22.010 --> 00:13:24.782
You don't make a lot more
money this day and time

00:13:24.782 --> 00:13:26.077
because it takes a lot more to live.

00:13:26.077 --> 00:13:27.403
- [Mark] And a lot of people
are living off the government.

00:13:27.403 --> 00:13:28.960
- Yes, yes.

00:13:28.960 --> 00:13:30.140
- [Mark] Because there's
no coal mining jobs

00:13:30.140 --> 00:13:32.600
and there's no other options, right?

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:34.260
What do you worry about?

00:13:34.260 --> 00:13:36.026
- Well, I don't worry about anything.

00:13:36.026 --> 00:13:41.026
You know, I love to see
my two grandkids raised.

00:13:41.310 --> 00:13:43.380
But I know they got a fine mom and dad.

00:13:43.380 --> 00:13:45.880
I know they will raise
them and take care of them.

00:13:47.111 --> 00:13:49.140
- [Mark] And what do your
daughters do for work?

00:13:49.140 --> 00:13:50.433
- They work for RoadSafe.

00:13:51.350 --> 00:13:52.183
- [Mark] Which is what?

00:13:52.183 --> 00:13:54.970
- Its a traffic system, they
flag for the power company.

00:13:54.970 --> 00:13:56.160
- [Mark] Okay.

00:13:56.160 --> 00:13:58.242
And where's your ex-wife

00:13:58.242 --> 00:14:00.123
after she got involved in drugs?

00:14:00.980 --> 00:14:02.350
- I guess she's on Toler.

00:14:02.350 --> 00:14:05.870
I don't, I don't have no
associations with them.

00:14:05.870 --> 00:14:06.720
- [Mark] She's on what?

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:09.370
- She lives on Toler Creek, they call it

00:14:09.370 --> 00:14:12.573
and, but I have no association with her.

00:14:13.820 --> 00:14:15.470
- [Mark] What's the most
important lesson you've learned

00:14:15.470 --> 00:14:16.303
in your life?

00:14:16.303 --> 00:14:20.050
- Well, how times, how hard times get.

00:14:20.050 --> 00:14:22.703
You know, times get hard sometimes.

00:14:22.703 --> 00:14:26.520
But you know, like I said, here
on the farm, we do our hogs.

00:14:26.520 --> 00:14:27.713
We do our chickens.

00:14:28.630 --> 00:14:31.420
We can live a long time,
but people would have to

00:14:31.420 --> 00:14:33.670
go to the store to get their next meal.

00:14:33.670 --> 00:14:35.420
I don't know what's going to happen

00:14:36.550 --> 00:14:39.320
but you got to keep going on and on

00:14:39.320 --> 00:14:42.000
because life goes on.

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:44.423
You know, you struggle for something,

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:48.223
it'll get better as time
goes, it'll get better.

00:14:49.870 --> 00:14:53.373
I just thank God that we've
got enough we can live.

00:14:56.116 --> 00:14:58.500
(rooster crowing)

00:14:58.500 --> 00:14:59.780
- [Mark] All right,
Randy, thank you so much

00:14:59.780 --> 00:15:01.950
for sharing your story.

00:15:01.950 --> 00:15:03.177
- Thank you for coming.

00:15:03.177 --> 00:15:04.285
- [Mark] And good luck
with your daughters,

00:15:04.285 --> 00:15:05.657
and your, the rest of your life.

00:15:05.657 --> 00:15:09.560
- And I want you to come
back, if it's just a visit

00:15:09.560 --> 00:15:10.700
when we kill hogs.

00:15:10.700 --> 00:15:14.080
I'll welcome you to come back
and eat some fresh hog meat

00:15:14.080 --> 00:15:17.200
on the old coal fire and enjoy with us.

00:15:17.200 --> 00:15:18.033
- [Mark] I'd love that.

00:15:18.033 --> 00:15:18.866
- [Randy] You don't have to family.

00:15:18.866 --> 00:15:21.370
I mean, you're welcome to come back

00:15:21.370 --> 00:15:23.612
and just sit around and
enjoy the food with us.

00:15:23.612 --> 00:15:24.445
(rooster crowing)

00:15:24.445 --> 00:15:25.278
- [Mark] That sounds wonderful.

00:15:25.278 --> 00:15:26.300
Thank you so much.

00:15:26.300 --> 00:15:28.000
- [Randy] Thank you for coming.

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:29.340
- [Mark] All right, Randy,
thank you very much.

00:15:29.340 --> 00:15:30.173
- [Randy] Thank you.

00:15:30.173 --> 00:15:31.278
- [Mark] All right.

